A||||12||N_Man||front end shake||||1||
Z||000000||N_Man||04-01-2000||11:15 PM||||my 87GT's front end likes to rock back and forth, shake, whatever you want to call it.<BR>It happens only at speeds above 60mph and when I hit a bump. The car will pull back and forth in the lane.<BR>There is also a slight clunk in the steering rack when i turn the wheel at a stop. I was thinking maybee my inner tie rod ends or my rack. Does anybody have any idea?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DiggityDan||04-01-2000||11:41 PM||||I have the same problem with mine.....I started looking today, and I found that my rack is shot. I wasn't for sure if it was the tie rod or the rack itself so I removed the boot so I could see the movement of the tie rod. Turned out it there was up and down play in the rack itself. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||04-02-2000||12:17 AM||||Tire size?  Wide ones will "hunt".<P>Shocks' condition?<P>Steering damper condition?<P>Overall tire condition? Cupped? Out of balance?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||04-02-2000||01:30 PM||||Usually the only part to go bad on the racks is the passenger side rack bushing. I have replacements on my web page. Easy to replace. No need to buy a rebuilt rack. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney/</A> <P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000004||DiggityDan||04-02-2000||02:04 PM||||Rodney......is it common for both sides to go out. My rack has play on both sides. I am wondering if this is the problem or if there is another problem. Also, if this is the problem, will your bushing fit both sides or just the passenger side. BTW, I don't have inner tie rod ends, just ball & socket type ends. Will your bushing kit still work for me. Also, what is the difference and which style is better if there is a difference. Also, my car is an 85 GT if that makes a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||04-02-2000||06:09 PM||||Dan, that ball and socket thingy that goes into both sides of the rack is the inner tie rod ends. They look totally different than the outer ones. These do wear out on high mileage Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||04-02-2000||08:39 PM||||If the passenger side bushing goes out it will cause the drivers side to move.Just replace the bushing and most likely you won't have any more problems.||||reg||1
Z||000007||N_Man||04-02-2000||08:52 PM||||I did a quick check.  My passenger side wheel has a little play in it.<BR>with the car sitting level on the ground I grabbed the top of the wheel and tugged, there was play and a slight clunk.<BR> <BR>I was wondering if this would be the bushing?||||reg||1
Z||000008||N_Man||04-02-2000||08:55 PM||||Rodney, your web page didnt work. Is there anywhere else I could try?||||reg||1
Z||000009||peterh||04-03-2000||08:29 AM||||I replaced all the front bushings, shocks, tie rod ends, etc. and got a rebuilt steering rack from the Fiero Store. I have driven the car less than 1000 miles since then. When I wiggle one of the front tires side to side (when jacked up) there is some play and I can see that the inner tie rod end boot covers are moving up and down a little. Is this normal or do I have bad inner tie rod ends or a bad rebuild on my steering rack. The place that did the front end alignment said the inner tie rod ends were ok. Sorry for the long message.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||RobertVol||04-04-2000||11:39 PM||||what yr? Did you check the ball joints. My 88 had alot of play on right side ....replaced the upper or was it hmmmm lower. oh well it was on the right side ball joint. once i replaced it ......NO PLAY ||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||04-05-2000||08:05 AM||||tie rod or wheel bearing wear can be checked by yanking the tire back and forth with your hands in the 3 and 9 o'clock position. If you have the car jacked up and there is play with your hands in the 12 and 6 o'clock position, that could be wheel bearings again or ball joints. The lower front ball joints USUALLY seem to wear the most on a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000012||peterh||04-05-2000||01:06 PM||||I forgot to mention that I also replaced both upper & lower ball joints on both sides. The car is an 86SE.<P>||||reg||1
